// RUN: %gopp check %S/Inputs/generic-refused.gopp
// RUN: not %gopp build -emit=gir %S/Inputs/generic-refused.gopp 2>&1 | FileCheck --implicit-check-not=error[E %s
// RUN: %gopp check %S/Inputs/generic-method-refused.gopp
// RUN: not %gopp build -emit=gir %S/Inputs/generic-method-refused.gopp 2>&1 | FileCheck --check-prefix=METHOD --implicit-check-not=error[E %s
// P04.08: what the lowering of generic code leaves to a later task is refused
// with one E0439 that names it, where it is, once however many times the code
// is instantiated and however often it is used: `@Export` on generic code and
// a type declared inside generic code (P04.28), and a method with type
// parameters of its own, of a plain or of a generic type (P04.28, reported by
// the instance walk where the method is declared, so the lowering of a call of
// it says nothing more). `gopp check` reports none of them (code generation
// is not its business). The other side: the same type declaration in a plain
// function lowers, a closure in generic code lowers per instance (decision
// M6; it was refused here until then), a generic function and a generic
// method that are never used draw nothing, and nothing else in the files
// draws an error.
// CHECK: :21:1: error[E0439]: an @Extern or @Export attribute on generic code (P04.28) is not supported
// CHECK: :7:7: error[E0439]: a type declared inside generic code (P04.28) is not supported
// METHOD: :7:1: error[E0439]: a generic method (P04.28) is not supported
// METHOD: :11:1: error[E0439]: a generic method (P04.28) is not supported
